[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug: the parser reads dates month first. Its reports hold three
problems with three different fixes. The parser fix that is queued closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser leaves the booking-v3 hint and the one-day shift open. Each has its own ticket below.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="vlunaklick/megadev" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, shift: 3 }} />

## What each one is

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads month first, and every tenant on file writes the day first.
  Day and month swap: 7 April becomes 4 July. Any channel.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants type
  month first because the form tells them to, and the form reads it day first. Only the `form` channel,
  only booking-v3 tenants. Fixing the parser does not fix this.
- **`shift`**: the date moves by one day, not day ↔ month. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
  Toronto got Monday." That points at a time zone conversion. A month-first parser cannot do this. The
  desk noted these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but `causes.ts` files them under `parser` by
  tenant, so the #4471 table counts them there.

## How it is counted

- The export pages overlap, so a report can come twice. One id, one report.
- Each report goes to one problem: a one-day move from the desk notes first, then `causeOf` by tenant.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the browser locale. Counting by tag gives a different, wrong split.
- The table is built from `reports-4471.json` at render time, so it follows the desk as it keeps merging.

